{"items":[{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p202754720","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-6osskgQRu4DnHzRbW49cyG","dateCreated":"2025-05-22T16:02:26+0000","dateUpdated":"2025-05-22T17:07:24+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eEC2 Public DNS names can now resolve to IPv6 Global Unicast Address (AAAA record) associated with your EC2 instances and Elastic Network Interfaces (ENI). This allows customers to publicly access their IPv6-enabled Amazon EC2 instances over IPv6, using EC2 Public DNS names.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nPrior to this, EC2 Public DNS name resolved to the Public IPv4 address (A record) associated with the primary ENI of the instance. So, customers adopting IPv6, used the specific IPv6 address instead of a DNS name to access an IPv6-only Amazon EC2 instance, or used a custom domain by creating a hosted zone using Amazon Route 53. IPv6 support for EC2 Public DNS names allows customers to easily access their IPv6-only Amazon EC2 instances, or formulate a migration plan that allows them to access a dual stack instance via IPv6, with a simple DNS cut over.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nThis feature is available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and customers can set IPv6 support for EC2 Public DNS using the same VPC settings that customers use to enable IPv4-only EC2 Public DNS name today. To learn more about using IPv6 support for EC2 Public DNS name, please refer to our \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-instance-naming.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2025/05/ipv6-support-ec2-public-dns-names/","postDateTime":"2025-05-22T10:00:00-07:00","headline":"AWS announces IPv6 support for EC2 Public DNS names "}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-ec2","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-ec2","description":"general:products/amazon-ec2","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2025","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2025","description":"\u003cp\u003e2025\u003c/p\u003e\n","dateCreated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#compute","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"compute","description":"marketing:marchitecture/compute","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p210029259","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-gmZu51K8GHGYeFhEBERWL8","dateCreated":"2025-04-07T18:51:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2025-04-17T18:10:42+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/memorydb/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAmazon MemoryDB\u003c/a\u003e clusters now support the IPv6 protocol, allowing clients to connect to MemoryDB clusters using IPv6. You can now configure your cluster to accept only IPv6 connections or to accept both IPv4 and IPv6 connections. This allows you to work to meet IPv6 compliance requirements and more efficiently integrate with existing IPv6-based applications.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nThe continued growth of the internet is rapidly depleting available Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) addresses. By supporting IPv6, MemoryDB helps customers simplify their network architecture by providing a significantly larger address space and eliminating the need to manage overlapping address spaces in their VPCs. Customers can now standardize their applications on IPv6 and future-proof their infrastructure while maintaining compatibility with existing IPv4 systems through dual-stack support.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nTo get started, create your new MemoryDB cluster using the Amazon Web Services Management Console, CLI, or SDKs and choose which protocol(s) it supports by setting its network type. IPv6 is supported when using Valkey 7 and above, Redis OSS version 6.2 and above, in all AWS global regions and at no additional cost.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nTo learn more about MemoryDB, visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/memorydb/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAmazon MemoryDB\u003c/a\u003e product page.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2025/04/amazon-memorydb-supports-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2025-04-17T10:00:00-07:00","headline":"Amazon MemoryDB now supports Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-memorydb","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-memorydb","description":"general:products/amazon-memorydb","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#databases","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"databases","description":"marketing:marchitecture/databases","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2025","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2025","description":"\u003cp\u003e2025\u003c/p\u003e\n","dateCreated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p212161090","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"oXSYKY6gS9gM8EUXE6cVUw","dateCreated":"2025-03-24T12:05:30+0000","dateUpdated":"2025-03-27T20:48:47+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Network Manager and AWS Cloud WAN now support AWS PrivateLink and IPv6 based connectivity to the management endpoint of these services. Using PrivateLink, customers can now access AWS Network Manager or AWS Cloud WAN privately on the AWS network, without going through the public Internet. Additionally, customers can now access these services over IPv6 using dual-stack endpoints.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nWith AWS Cloud WAN, you can use a central dashboard and network policies to create a global network that spans multiple locations and networks, allowing you to configure and manage different networks using the same technology. The Cloud WAN central dashboard, powered by AWS Network Manager, generates a complete view of the network to help you monitor network health, security, and performance. AWS Network Manager reduces the operational complexity of managing global networks across AWS and on-premises locations. Previously, you could access AWS Cloud WAN and AWS Network Manager using public IPv4 endpoints only. With this launch, you can now access these services\u0026rsquo; APIs/CLI privately, without going through the public Internet. Additionally, these services now support IPv6 endpoints.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nTo learn more about AWS Network Manager, refer \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-manager/latest/tgwnm/what-are-global-networks.html#nm-privatelink\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e, and for AWS Cloud WAN, refer \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-manager/latest/cloudwan/what-is-cloudwan.html#cloudwan-privatelink\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2025/03/aws-network-manager-cloud-wan-privatelink-ipv6","postDateTime":"2025-03-27T16:50:00-04:00","headline":"AWS Network Manager and AWS Cloud WAN now support AWS PrivateLink and IPv6"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-privatelink","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-privatelink","description":"general:products/aws-privatelink","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2025","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2025","description":"\u003cp\u003e2025\u003c/p\u003e\n","dateCreated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-cloud-wan","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-cloud-wan","description":"general:products/aws-cloud-wan","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#d192749474","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-uNyGTMWnVdopBVDHpL6WUr","dateCreated":"2025-01-29T20:37:13+0000","dateUpdated":"2025-01-30T19:34:09+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch Synthetics now allows canaries running in a VPC to make outbound requests to IPv6 endpoints allowing monitoring of IPv6-only and dual stack enabled endpoints over IPv6. You can also access CloudWatch Synthetics APIs over both IPv4 and IPv6 through new dual stack compatible regional endpoints. Additionally, PrivateLink access to Synthetics within VPCs is now available over IPv6 connections.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nUsing CloudWatch Synthetics, you can now monitor the availability and performance of websites or microservices accessible via IPv6 endpoints ensuring that end users can use the applications seamlessly irrespective of their network protocol. You can create IPv6 enabled canaries in your VPC using the CLI, CDK, CloudFormation, or the AWS console, and update existing VPC canaries to support dual stack connectivity without making any script changes. You can monitor endpoints external to your VPC by giving the canary internet access and configuring the VPC subnets appropriately. Now you can manage Synthetics resources in environments with IPv6-only networking policies, or access Synthetics APIs via IPv6 without traffic traversing the internet using PrivateLink helping meet security and regulatory requirements.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nIPv6 support for Synthetics is available in all commercial regions where CloudWatch Synthetics is present at no additional cost to the users.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nTo learn how to configure a IPv6 canary in a VPC see \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/CloudWatch_Synthetics_Canaries_VPC.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e, or click \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/cw-synthetics_region.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e to find dual-stack API management endpoints for Synthetics. See \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/CloudWatch_Synthetics_Canaries.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003euser guide\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://catalog.workshops.aws/observability/en-US/aws-native/app-monitoring/synthetics\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eOne Observability Workshop\u003c/a\u003e to get started with CloudWatch Synthetics.\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2025/01/amazon-cloudwatch-synthetics-ipv6-support/","postDateTime":"2025-01-30T10:00:00-08:00","headline":"Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics adds IPv6 support"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#developer-tools","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"developer-tools","description":"marketing:marchitecture/developer-tools","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-cloudwatch","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-cloudwatch","description":"general:products/amazon-cloudwatch","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:47+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:47+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#management-and-governance","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"management-and-governance","description":"marketing:marchitecture/management-and-governance","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T19:30:55+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T19:30:55+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2025","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2025","description":"\u003cp\u003e2025\u003c/p\u003e\n","dateCreated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-30T16:07:22+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p177773020","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-2r8KX7vECBquQVT5paU7pJ","dateCreated":"2024-12-17T19:53:11+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-20T18:45:30+0000","additionalFields":{"headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/12/amazon-location-service-ipv6/","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Location Service announces the availability of dual-stack endpoints, enabling customers to connect using Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6), Internet Protocol Version 4 (IPv4), or dual-stack clients. These dual-stack endpoints are accessible through a new AWS DNS domain name, while existing Amazon Location Service endpoints remain available to ensure backward compatibility.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nThe transition to IPv6 is essential due to the ongoing expansion of the internet, which is rapidly depleting available IPv4 addresses. With simultaneous support for both IPv4 and IPv6 on Amazon Location Service endpoints, customers can gradually migrate their systems and applications to IPv6-based architectures as needed. This flexibility allows businesses to meet IPv6 compliance requirements while maintaining uninterrupted connectivity through IPv4 for those not yet utilizing IPv6.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nIPv6 support for Amazon Location Service is now available in all regions where the service is generally available (GA).\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nSee \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/location.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e for a full list of supported endpoints and instructions on how to access them via \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-endpoints.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAWS SDKs and CLI\u003c/a\u003e. To learn more about Amazon Location Service, please visit \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/location/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAmazon Location Service Product Page\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/location/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edeveloper guide\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n","postDateTime":"2024-12-20T10:00:00-08:00","headline":"Amazon Location Service now supports Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) "}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#mobile-services","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"mobile-services","description":"marketing:marchitecture/mobile-services","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-09-17T18:03:36+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-location-service","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-location-service","description":"general:products/amazon-location-service","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:05:54+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:05:54+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p175511190","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-tpLX3dMuCKQu2yxXqVr3fG","dateCreated":"2024-12-09T20:32:13+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-12-19T20:36:45+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eToday, AWS announces Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) support for Recycle Bin, a data recovery feature that enables restoration of accidental deleted Amazon EBS Snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs. You now have the option of using IPv6 addresses for new and existing endpoints. By moving to IPv6, you can simplify your network stack by running dual-stack Recycle Bin endpoints on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nCustomers can create rules in Recycle Bin to retain deleted EBS Snapshots or deregistered EBS-backed AMI for a specific retention time. This capability allows you to immediately recover your deleted snapshots or EBS-backed AMIs when you create volumes or launch instance without a need to roll back to a snapshot or AMI from an earlier point in time. Recovered snapshots or AMIs retain attributes such as prior to deletion and can be used immediately for creating volumes or launching instances. Snapshots and AMIs that are not recovered from the Recycle Bin are permanently deleted upon expiration of the retention time.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nRecycle Bin with IPv6 and AWS PrivateLink is now available in all AWS Regions including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nTo learn more about configuring Recycle Bin endpoints for IPv6, please refer to our \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/rbin-service-endpoints.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/12/ipv6-support-recycle-bin/","postDateTime":"2024-12-19T10:00:00-08:00","headline":"Announcing Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) support for Recycle Bin"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#storage","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"storage","description":"marketing:marchitecture/storage","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-09-17T18:03:36+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-ebs-snapshots-archive","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-ebs-snapshots-archive","description":"general:products/amazon-ebs-snapshots-archive","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:37:11+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:37:11+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-privatelink","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-privatelink","description":"general:products/aws-privatelink","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p153851510","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"oGeSRX76wCkrEM1acZ28nv","dateCreated":"2024-09-16T18:51:45+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-09-25T17:00:24+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/serverless/serverlessrepo/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAWS Serverless Application Repository\u003c/a\u003e now supports \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/security-private-link.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAWS PrivateLink\u003c/a\u003e to connect to AWS Serverless Application Repository through an interface VPC endpoint. You can now connect directly to the AWS Serverless Application Repository using AWS PrivateLink in your virtual private cloud (VPC) instead of connecting over the internet.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nWhen you use an AWS PrivateLink, communication between your VPC and AWS Serverless Application Repository is conducted entirely within the AWS network, which can provide greater security and protect your sensitive information. An AWS PrivateLink endpoint connects your VPC directly to AWS Serverless Application Repository. The instances in your VPC don\u0026#39;t need public IP addresses to communicate with the AWS Serverless Application Repository API. To use AWS Serverless Application Repository through your VPC, you have two options. One is to connect from an instance that is inside your VPC. The other is to connect your private network to your VPC by using an AWS VPN option or AWS Direct Connect. You can create an AWS PrivateLink to connect to AWS Serverless Application Repository using the AWS Management Console or AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) commands. For more information, see \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonVPC/latest/UserGuide/vpce-interface.html#create-interface-endpoint\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eCreating an Interface Endpoint\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nAWS Serverless Application Repository support for AWS PrivateLink is available in all \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eAWS regions\u003c/a\u003e where AWS Serverless Application Repository is available.\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/09/aws-serverless-application-repository-privatelink","postDateTime":"2024-09-25T13:00:00-04:00","contentType":"launch announcement","headline":"AWS Serverless Application Repository now supports AWS PrivateLink"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-09-17T18:03:36+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#developer-tools","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"developer-tools","description":"marketing:marchitecture/developer-tools","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p148363304","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"8GQGYz5iyeSQUbB1qum3hb","dateCreated":"2024-08-19T15:42:32+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-08-19T20:33:48+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Lightsail now extends support for browser-based SSH/RDP connections, also known as Lightsail Connect, to IPv6-only instances. Now, you can connect to your Linux or Windows IPv6-only instance directly from Lightsail Console in browser.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nPreviously, you would need to add a public IPv4 address to your IPv6-only instance to use Lightsail Connect. With the launch of IPv6 support, you can use Lightsail\u0026rsquo;s browser-based client that provides a quick and convenient way to connect to your instance and does not require any software installation.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nYou can use this feature on the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://lightsail.aws.amazon.com/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eLightsail Console\u003c/a\u003e (accessed from \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/console/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003e\u003cu\u003eAWS Console\u003c/u\u003e\u003c/a\u003e) in \u003ca href\u003d\"https://lightsail.aws.amazon.com/ls/docs/en_us/articles/understanding-regions-and-availability-zones-in-amazon-lightsail\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eall AWS Regions supporting Lightsail\u003c/a\u003e. To learn more about connecting to your instance from the Lightsail console, please refer to \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lightsail/latest/userguide/lightsail-how-to-connect-to-your-instance-virtual-private-server.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/08/browser-based-ssh-rdp-ipv6-only-instances-bundles-lightsail","postDateTime":"2024-08-19T16:30:00-04:00","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Introducing browser-based SSH/RDP support for IPv6-only instances bundles on Lightsail"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#compute","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"compute","description":"marketing:marchitecture/compute","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-lightsail","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-lightsail","description":"general:products/amazon-lightsail","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:09+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:09+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p144358936","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"b9FcPaVGHvu22iGPdi2Xgj","dateCreated":"2024-08-05T19:26:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-08-08T19:50:08+0000","additionalFields":{"nofollow":"0","noindex":"0","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAWS announces the general availability of private IPv6 addressing for VPCs and subnets with Amazon VPC IP Address Manager (IPAM). On AWS, private IPv6 addresses can take the form of Unique Local IPv6 Unicast Addresses (ULA) and Global Unicast Addresses (GUA), and can only be used for private access. These IPv6 addresses are not and cannot be advertised to the internet by AWS. Within IPAM, customers can configure private IPv6 addresses in a private scope, provision ULA and GUA, and use them to create VPCs and subnets for private access.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nCustomers want private IPv6 address for the innate security boost it offers as resources using private IPv6 address cannot access the internet directly. It also provides assurance for compliance as customers can demonstrate that their resources with private IPv6 addresses are not internet accessible through a quick audit. These customers have no intention of directly routing traffic from these resources to the internet via AWS, and instead use proxies or network appliances for selective internet access via AWS, or route traffic through their on-premise network where the IPv6 address range is advertised to the internet. For such use cases, private IPv6 addressing helps simplify IP addressing and VPC network configuration in IPv6.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nPrivate IPv6 addressing for VPCs and subnets is now available in all AWS commercial regions and AWS GovCloud (US) regions, in both Free Tier and Advanced Tier VPC IPAM. To learn more about IPv6 addressing type, see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/understanding-ipv6-addressing-on-aws-and-designing-a-scalable-addressing-plan\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eblog\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-ip-addressing.html#vpc-ipv6-addresses\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eVPC documentation\u003c/a\u003e, and to get started, please see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/ipam/intro-create-ipv6-pools.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eIPAM documentation\u003c/a\u003e page.\u003c/p\u003e\n","nosnippet":"0","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/08/aws-private-ipv6-addressing-vpcs-subnets","postDateTime":"2024-08-08T15:30:00-04:00","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS announces private IPv6 addressing for VPCs and subnets"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#p131405956","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"launch-pLtPWAdJLtvjsAHWYs1r1n","dateCreated":"2024-07-08T20:45:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-15T17:04:24+0000","additionalFields":{"headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/07/aws-cloud-control-api-ipv6/","postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Cloud Control API now allows customers to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses for their new and existing service endpoints. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their AWS Cloud Control API endpoints on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nThe continued growth of the Internet, particularly in the areas of mobile applications, connected devices, and IoT, has spurred an industry-wide move to IPv6. IPv6 increases the number of available addresses by several orders of magnitude so customers will no longer need to manage overlapping address spaces in their VPCs. Customers can standardize their applications on the new version of Internet Protocol by moving their AWS Cloud Control API endpoints to IPv6 with AWS CLI.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u003cbr /\u003e\nSupport for IPv6 on AWS Cloud Control API is available in all Regions where AWS Cloud Control API is available. See \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/cloudcontrolapi.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e for a full listing of our Regions. To learn more about AWS Cloud Control API, please refer to our \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cloudcontrolapi/latest/userguide/what-is-cloudcontrolapi.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003euser guide\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr /\u003e\n\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n","postDateTime":"2024-07-15T10:00:00-07:00","headline":"AWS Cloud Control API now supports IPv6"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#developer-tools","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"developer-tools","description":"marketing:marchitecture/developer-tools","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2024-03-amazon-vpc-dhcpv6-setting-ipv6-lease-time","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/03/amazon-vpc-dhcpv6-setting-ipv6-lease-time/","dateCreated":"2024-03-07T22:50:46+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T15:02:12+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eToday, Amazon VPC added a new feature to its DHCP option set called IPv6 preferred lease time setting, which allows customers to adjust the frequency of IPv6 lease renewals. This feature gives customers the option to increase the preferred lease time of IPv6 address assignments on \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ec2/latest/instancetypes/ec2-nitro-instances.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon EC2 Nitro instances\u003c/a\u003e to minimize the number of IPv6 lease renewals and prevent possible renewal failures. This setting applies to both dualstack and IPv6-only Amazon EC2 Nitro instances in the VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EC2 instances use DHCP lease renewals to update IPv6 address assignment on a periodic basis. By default, the IPv6 preferred lease time is 140 seconds and DHCP lease renewals for IPv6 address assignments occur at the halfway mark of 70 seconds. Customers who require long-term IPv6 addressing for their Amazon EC2 Nitro instances can increase the IPv6 preferred lease time and avoid frequent renewals. The IPv6 preferred lease time for Amazon EC2 instances other than Amazon EC2 Nitro instances will remain 140 seconds.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThe IPv6 preferred lease time setting is available in all AWS commercial regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the Amazon Web Services China (Beijing) Region, operated by Sinnet and Amazon Web Services China (Ningxia) Region, operated by NWCD. Customers can use the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, and APIs for this feature. For more information on this feature, visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/DHCPOptionSet.html#CreatingaDHCPOptionSet\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eVPC documentation page\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2024-03-07T22:50:26Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/03/amazon-vpc-dhcpv6-setting-ipv6-lease-time/","postDateTime":"2024-03-07T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Announcing new Amazon VPC DHCPv6 setting to adjust IPv6 preferred lease time","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eToday, Amazon VPC added a new feature to its DHCP option set called IPv6 preferred lease time setting, which allows customers to adjust the frequency of IPv6 lease renewals. This feature gives customers the option to increase the preferred lease time of IPv6 address assignments on \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ec2/latest/instancetypes/ec2-nitro-instances.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon EC2 Nitro instances\u003c/a\u003e to minimize the number of IPv6 lease renewals and prevent possible renewal failures. This setting applies to both dualstack and IPv6-only Amazon EC2 Nitro instances in the VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2024-02-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/02/amazon-cloudwatch-logs-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2024-02-26T20:41:36+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T15:02:09+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Logs now allows customers to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses for their new and existing domains. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their CloudWatch log groups on a dual-stack network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eIPv6 addresses global increase in the demand for internet connected devices, without the need for NAT and is largely scalable. Customers can standardize their applications on the new version of Internet Protocol by moving their log groups to IPv6 using the CloudWatch Logs console or APIs.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eSupport for IPv6 on CloudWatch Logs service is available in all \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regions_az/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Commercial regions\u003c/a\u003e.\u0026nbsp;With a few clicks in the AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) or AWS SDKs you can start using CloudWatch Logs IPv6 to send logs to CloudWatch.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo learn more about configuring your environment for IPv6, please refer to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/cwl_region.html#cwl_region\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eCloudWatch Logs API Reference page\u003c/a\u003e To learn more about CloudWatch Logs, please visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/features/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eproduct page\u003c/a\u003e.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2024-02-29T17:31:07Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/02/amazon-cloudwatch-logs-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2024-02-26T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon CloudWatch Logs now supports IPv6 ","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Logs now allows customers to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses for their new and existing domains. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their CloudWatch log groups on a dual-stack network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-cloudwatch-logs","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-cloudwatch-logs","description":"general:products/amazon-cloudwatch-logs","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:56:30+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:56:30+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#management-and-governance","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"management-and-governance","description":"marketing:marchitecture/management-and-governance","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T19:30:55+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T19:30:55+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2024-01-amazon-s3-outposts-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/01/amazon-s3-outposts-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2024-02-02T13:43:47+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T15:00:33+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eYou can now access Amazon S3 on Outposts buckets using IPv6 via S3 on Outposts dual-stack endpoints. IPv6 support for S3 on Outposts allows you to manage your S3 on Outposts buckets and control plane resources over IPv6 networks.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThis new feature expands IP addressing capabilities and simplifies network configuration, helping you to build a simple hybrid cloud storage architecture across IPv4 and IPv6 networks. You can also use source address filtering in IAM and bucket policies with IPv6 addresses to restrict access to S3 on Outposts buckets from specific IPv6 applications. Additionally,  you can use IPv6 over AWS PrivateLink, which enables secure, private communication between IPv6 services and VPCs, without traversing the public internet.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eS3 on Outposts IPv6 support is available in all AWS Regions where S3 on Outposts is available, including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, at no additional cost. You can connect via CLI and SDK over both IPv4 and IPv6 protocols. Read more about how to use \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/S3OutpostsIPv6-access.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eIPv6 for S3 on Outposts\u003c/a\u003e in our documentation.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2024-01-16T18:23:16Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2024/01/amazon-s3-outposts-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2024-01-16T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon S3 on Outposts enables support for IPv6","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eYou can now access Amazon S3 on Outposts buckets using IPv6 via S3 on Outposts dual-stack endpoints. IPv6 support for S3 on Outposts allows you to manage your S3 on Outposts buckets and control plane resources over IPv6 networks.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2024","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2024","description":"year#2024","dateCreated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-01-02T21:28:59+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#storage","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"storage","description":"marketing:marchitecture/storage","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T20:59:48+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#compute","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"compute","description":"marketing:marchitecture/compute","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-s3","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-s3","description":"general:products/amazon-s3","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:07+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:07+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-11-aws-ipv6-tiered-vpcs-subnets","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/aws-ipv6-tiered-vpcs-subnets/","dateCreated":"2023-11-16T20:45:01+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-16T20:45:02+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eCustomers require tiered VPCs for growth planning, and can use a single contiguous CIDR block across thousands of smaller (E.g., /60) VPCs to optimize CIDR allocations. Alternatively, customers that want to match on-premise network designs of a logical network can create a large (E.g., /44) VPC and have a subnet of the same size in it.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eIPv6 tiered VPCs and subnets is now available in all AWS commercial regions and AWS GovCloud (US) regions. For more information, please visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/create-vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eVPC documentation page\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-11-16T20:44:44Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/aws-ipv6-tiered-vpcs-subnets/","postDateTime":"2023-11-16T13:00:00Z","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAWS announces IPv6 tiered VPCs and subnets that allows customers to create VPCs and subnets of different sizes using IPv6 CIDRs. With this capability, customers can now create VPCs in sizes between /44 and /60, and subnets in sizes between /44 and /64, in increments of /4. Before today, AWS supported one standard IPv6 CIDR block size of /56 for VPC and /64 for subnet, whereas IPv4 CIDR block size were flexible for both VPCs and subnets.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS announces IPv6 tiered VPCs and subnets"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-11-vpcs-subnets-support-more-sizes-ipv6-cidrs","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/vpcs-subnets-support-more-sizes-ipv6-cidrs/","dateCreated":"2023-11-16T22:47:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:49:27+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon VPC allows customers to create VPCs and subnets of different sizes using IPv6 CIDRs. With this capability, customers can now create VPCs in sizes between /44 and /60, and subnets in sizes between /44 and /64, in increments of /4. Before today, AWS supported one standard IPv6 CIDR block size of /56 for VPC and /64 for subnet, whereas IPv4 CIDR block size were flexible for both VPCs and subnets.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eCustomers require VPCs of different sizes for growth planning, and can use a single contiguous CIDR block across thousands of smaller (E.g., /60) VPCs to optimize CIDR allocations. Alternatively, customers that want to match on-premise network designs of a logical network can create a large (E.g., /44) VPC and have a subnet of the same size in it.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eVPCs and subnets of different sizes using IPv6 CIDRs is now available in all AWS commercial regions and AWS GovCloud (US) regions. For more information,\u0026nbsp;please visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/create-vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eVPC documentation page\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-11-16T23:21:19Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/vpcs-subnets-support-more-sizes-ipv6-cidrs/","postDateTime":"2023-11-16T13:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"VPCs and subnets now support more sizes for IPv6 CIDRs","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon VPC allows customers to create VPCs and subnets of different sizes using IPv6 CIDRs. With this capability, customers can now create VPCs in sizes between /44 and /60, and subnets in sizes between /44 and /64, in increments of /4. Before today, AWS supported one standard IPv6 CIDR block size of /56 for VPC and /64 for subnet, whereas IPv4 CIDR block size were flexible for both VPCs and subnets.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-11-aws-global-accelerator-ipv6-dual-stack-nlb-endpoints","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/aws-global-accelerator-ipv6-dual-stack-nlb-endpoints/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T15:08:06+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:59:30+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eStarting today, AWS Global Accelerator extends dual-stack accelerators support that enables you to route IPv6 traffic to Regional Network Load Balancer endpoints, in addition to dual-stack Application Load Balancer (ALB) and EC2 endpoints.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith dual-stack accelerators, you can route IPv6 traffic, together with IPv4 traffic, towards dual-stack Network Load Balancer endpoints. With dual-stack accelerators, you will get two static IPv6 addresses, in addition to the two static IPv4 addresses. You can choose a dual-stack accelerator when you set up a new accelerator, or you can update an existing IPv4-only accelerator to dual-stack, using standard AWS tools, including the AWS Management Console, AWS SDK, or the AWS CLI. When you update an existing accelerator, there\u0027s no change to the IPv4 specifications of the accelerator. You can monitor your IPv6 traffic in addition to the IPv4 traffic with existing Global Accelerator CloudWatch metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThere is no additional charge when you use dual-stack accelerators in Global Accelerator. To learn more, see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/latest/dg/what-is-global-accelerator.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edeveloper documentation\u003c/a\u003e in the AWS Global Accelerator Developer Guide. To get started with AWS Global Accelerator, visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eGlobal Accelerator product page\u003c/a\u003e.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-11-02T20:11:41Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/11/aws-global-accelerator-ipv6-dual-stack-nlb-endpoints/","postDateTime":"2023-11-02T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Global Accelerator extends IPv6 support to dual stack NLB endpoints","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eStarting today, AWS Global Accelerator extends dual-stack accelerators support that enables you to route IPv6 traffic to Regional Network Load Balancer endpoints, in addition to dual-stack Application Load Balancer (ALB) and EC2 endpoints.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-global-accelerator","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-global-accelerator","description":"general:products/aws-global-accelerator","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:54:44+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:54:44+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-08-amazon-vpc-primary-ipv6-address-elastic-interface","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/08/amazon-vpc-primary-ipv6-address-elastic-interface/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T15:08:50+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:58:18+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Web Services (AWS) announces the launch of primary IPv6 address in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), enabling customer to make the first IPv6 address associated with their elastic network interface (ENI) immutable. Once the first IPv6 address is made primary on the ENI, the IPv6 address cannot be removed as long as the ENI is attached to an instance or until the instance is terminated, effectively making the address immutable. The primary IPv6 address can be an Amazon provided IPv6 address or a Bring your own IP (IPv6) addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith IPv4, customers use the primary IPv4 address on the instance to route traffic, because the primary IPv4 address on the instance is immutable for the lifetime of the instance. This way adding or removing IPv4 addresses to the ENI will not impact traffic flows or connectivity. In IPv6, customers can use primary IPv6 feature to set a primary IPv6 address on the instance to route traffic without worrying about the IPv6 address changing.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThis feature is available in all AWS commercial and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no additional cost. For more information, please visit the VPC \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/using-eni.html#working-with-enis\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003euser guide\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-08-04T21:24:49Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/08/amazon-vpc-primary-ipv6-address-elastic-interface/","postDateTime":"2023-08-04T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon VPC now supports primary IPv6 address on an elastic network interface ","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Web Services (AWS) announces the launch of primary IPv6 address in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), enabling customer to make the first IPv6 address associated with their elastic network interface (ENI) immutable. Once the first IPv6 address is made primary on the ENI, the IPv6 address cannot be removed as long as the ENI is attached to an instance or until the instance is terminated, effectively making the address immutable. The primary IPv6 address can be an Amazon provided IPv6 address or a Bring your own IP (IPv6) addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-05-amazon-athena-ipv6-endpoints-inbound-connections","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/05/amazon-athena-ipv6-endpoints-inbound-connections/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T15:05:39+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:34:08+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/a\u003e has expanded its support for inbound connections via Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) endpoints to include \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/privatelink/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS PrivateLink\u003c/a\u003e. Starting today, you can now connect to Athena securely and privately using PrivateLink from your \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Virtual Private Cloud\u003c/a\u003e (VPC), in addition to the public IPv6 endpoints that were previously available.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThe rapid growth of the Internet is exhausting the availability of Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) addresses. IPv6 increases the number of available addresses by several times, and customers no longer have to manage overlapping address spaces in their VPCs. With this release, you can now combine the benefits of IPv6 addressing, such as a larger address space, with the security and performance advantages of PrivateLink.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo connect programmatically to an AWS service, you can specify an endpoint in \u003ca href\u003d\"https://awscli.amazonaws.com/v2/documentation/api/latest/reference/index.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS CLI\u003c/a\u003e or \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/getting-started/tools-sdks/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS SDK\u003c/a\u003e. For more information on service endpoints, see \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/rande.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Service endpoints\u003c/a\u003e.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-05-19T16:41:43Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/05/amazon-athena-ipv6-endpoints-inbound-connections/","postDateTime":"2023-05-18T12:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon Athena now supports IPv6 endpoints for inbound connections","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/athena.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/a\u003e has expanded its support for inbound connections via Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) endpoints to include \u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/privatelink.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS PrivateLink\u003c/a\u003e. Starting today, you can now connect to Athena securely and privately using PrivateLink from your \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Virtual Private Cloud\u003c/a\u003e (VPC), in addition to the public IPv6 endpoints that were previously available.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-athena","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-athena","description":"general:products/amazon-athena","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:35+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#analytics","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"analytics","description":"marketing:marchitecture/analytics","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:36:01+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:36:01+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-04-aws-network-firewall-ipv6-only-subnets","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/04/aws-network-firewall-ipv6-only-subnets/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:45:02+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:52:43+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Network Firewall\u003c/a\u003e now supports IPv6-only subnets, in addition to dual stack (IPv4 and IPv6) subnets. Starting today, you can filter IPv6 traffic to and from the public internet, on-premises network, or any endpoint in your IPv6-enabled Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with IPv6-only subnets.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Network Firewall is a managed firewall service that makes it easy to deploy essential network protections for all your Amazon VPCs. IPv6-only subnet support allows you to enforce your AWS Network Firewall rules and policies consistently across your entire network, making it easier to manage your network security posture. Additionally, with this launch, you will be able to meet business or regulatory requirements for adoption of IPv6-only network environments on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eIPv6 is supported in all AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall is available today, including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information about the AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall is available, see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Region table\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThere is no additional cost to enable IPv6-only AWS Network Firewall endpoints. You can configure IPv6 firewall endpoints using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDK, or the AWS Network Firewall API. To learn more about configuring AWS Network Firewall for IPv6-only subnets, please refer to the service \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-04-06T16:29:09Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/04/aws-network-firewall-ipv6-only-subnets/","postDateTime":"2023-04-05T12:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Network Firewall now supports IPv6-only subnets","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/network-firewall.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Network Firewall\u003c/a\u003e now supports IPv6-only subnets, in addition to dual stack (IPv4 and IPv6) subnets. Starting today, you can filter IPv6 traffic to and from the public internet, on-premises network, or any endpoint in your IPv6-enabled Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with IPv6-only subnets.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#security-identity-and-compliance","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"security-identity-and-compliance","description":"marketing:marchitecture/security-identity-and-compliance","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-network-firewall","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-network-firewall","description":"general:products/aws-network-firewall","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:57:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:57:41+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2023-01-aws-network-firewall-ipv6-support","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/01/aws-network-firewall-ipv6-support/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:46:52+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:51:19+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Network Firewall\u003c/a\u003e now supports IPv6 for dual stack subnets so you can filter IPv4 and IPv6 traffic flows to and from the public internet, on-premises network, or any endpoint in your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). Now, you can use AWS Network Firewall to protect your IPv6 workloads on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Network Firewall is a managed firewall service that makes it easy to deploy essential network protections for all your Amazon VPCs. With this capability, you can enable AWS Network Firewall endpoints to filter both IPv4 and IPv6 traffic in dual stack subnets. A dual stack subnet is a subnet with both an IPv4 CIDR block and an IPv6 CIDR block. Resources in a dual-stack subnet can communicate over IPv4 and IPv6.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThere is no additional cost to enable dual stack AWS Network Firewall endpoints. You can configure dual stack firewall endpoints using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDK, or the AWS Network Firewall API. IPv6 is supported in all AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall is available today, including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information on availability, please see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Region table\u003c/a\u003e. To learn more about configuring AWS Network Firewall for dual stack, please refer to the service \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-01-18T00:07:27Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2023/01/aws-network-firewall-ipv6-support/","postDateTime":"2023-01-17T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Network Firewall announces IPv6 support ","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/network-firewall.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Network Firewall\u003c/a\u003e now supports IPv6 for dual stack subnets so you can filter IPv4 and IPv6 traffic flows to and from the public internet, on-premises network, or any endpoint in your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). Now, you can use AWS Network Firewall to protect your IPv6 workloads on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2023","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2023","description":"year#2023","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-network-firewall","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-network-firewall","description":"general:products/aws-network-firewall","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:57:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:57:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-govcloud-us","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-govcloud-us","description":"general:products/aws-govcloud-us","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:41:18+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#security-identity-and-compliance","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"security-identity-and-compliance","description":"marketing:marchitecture/security-identity-and-compliance","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:49+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-11-aws-database-migration-service-supports-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/11/aws-database-migration-service-supports-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:52:29+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:37:51+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/a\u003e now offers customers the option to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses in their \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/vpc/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003eVirtual Private Cloud (VPC)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cspan\u003e on new and existing DMS Replication Instances. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their DMS Replication Instances on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThe continued growth of the Internet is exhausting available Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) addresses. IPv6 increases the number of available addresses several fold and customers will no longer need to manage overlapping address spaces in their VPCs. Customers can standardize their applications on the new version of Internet Protocol by moving to IPv6 with a few clicks in the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo learn more about configuring your environment for IPv6, please refer to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_ReplicationInstance.IPAddressing.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eDMS Technical Documentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2023-02-08T19:42:41Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/11/aws-database-migration-service-supports-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2022-11-18T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Database Migration Service now supports Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6)","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/dms.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/a\u003e now offers customers the option to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses in their \u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003eVirtual Private Cloud (VPC)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cspan\u003e on new and existing DMS Replication Instances. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their DMS Replication Instances on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-database-migration-service","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-database-migration-service","description":"general:products/aws-database-migration-service","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:54:46+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:54:46+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#databases","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"databases","description":"marketing:marchitecture/databases","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#developer-tools","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"developer-tools","description":"marketing:marchitecture/developer-tools","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#migration","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"migration","description":"marketing:marchitecture/migration","dateCreated":"2023-11-08T19:09:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-08T19:09:35+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-11-amazon-elasticache-supports-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/11/amazon-elasticache-supports-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:07+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:50:44+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon ElastiCache\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;clusters now support the IPv6 protocol, allowing clients to connect to ElastiCache clusters using IPv6. You can now configure your cluster to accept only IPv6 connections or to accept both IPv4 and IPv6 connections. This allows you to work to meet IPv6 compliance requirements and more efficiently integrate with existing IPv6-based applications.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo get started, create your new ElastiCache cluster using the Amazon Web Services Management Console, CLI, or SDKs and choose which protocol(s) it supports by setting its network type. IPv6 is supported when using Redis engine version 6.2 and above and Memcached version 1.6.6 and above, in all AWS global regions and at no additional cost.\u0026nbsp;To learn more, see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/red-ug/SelectEngine.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;for Redis or the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/mem-ug/WhatIs.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;for Memcached.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-11-08T21:08:07Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/11/amazon-elasticache-supports-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2022-11-07T08:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon ElastiCache now supports Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/elasticache.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon ElastiCache\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;clusters now support the IPv6 protocol, allowing clients to connect to ElastiCache clusters using IPv6. You can now configure your cluster to accept only IPv6 connections or to accept both IPv4 and IPv6 connections. This allows you to work to meet IPv6 compliance requirements and more efficiently integrate with existing IPv6-based applications.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-elasticache","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-elasticache","description":"general:products/amazon-elasticache","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T20:20:48+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T20:20:48+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#databases","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"databases","description":"marketing:marchitecture/databases","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-10-aws-database-migration-service-supports-c6i-r6i-instances","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/10/aws-database-migration-service-supports-c6i-r6i-instances/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:53:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:46:02+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/a\u003e now\u0026nbsp;supports Amazon EC2 C6i and R6i instance types. These instances are powered by 3rd Generation Intel Xeon Scalable processors with an all-core turbo frequency of 3.5 GHz, offering up to 15% better compute price performance over comparable Generation5 instances for a wide variety of workloads, and always-on memory encryption using Intel Total Memory Encryption (TME).\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eDesigned for a wide variety of workloads, C6i and R6i instances are built on the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/nitro/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Nitro System\u003c/a\u003e, a combinationof dedicated hardware and lightweight hypervisor, which delivers practically all of the compute and memory resources of the host hardware to your instances. C6i instances are an ideal fit for compute-intensive workloads and R6i instances are an ideal fit for memory intensive workloads. To meet customer demands for increased scalability, these instances provide new 32xlarge size with 128 vCPUs, 33% more than the largest Generation5 instance. They also give customers up to 50 Gbps of networking speed and 40 Gbps of bandwidth to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/ebs/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Elastic Block Store\u003c/a\u003e, twice that of Generation5 instances.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo get started, visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://console.aws.amazon.com/dms/v2/home?region\u003dus-east-1#dashboard\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS DMS Console\u003c/a\u003e, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/dms/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS DMS Command Line Interface (CLI)\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/latest/AWS/DMS.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS SDKs\u003c/a\u003e. For complete information on pricing and regional availability, please refer to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/pricing/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS DMS pricing page\u003c/a\u003e.\u0026nbsp;\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-10-17T21:01:38Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/10/aws-database-migration-service-supports-c6i-r6i-instances/","postDateTime":"2022-10-17T12:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Database Migration Service now supports C6i and R6i instances","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/dms.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/a\u003e now\u0026nbsp;supports Amazon EC2 C6i and R6i instance types. These instances are powered by 3rd Generation Intel Xeon Scalable processors with an all-core turbo frequency of 3.5 GHz, offering up to 15% better compute price performance over comparable Generation5 instances for a wide variety of workloads, and always-on memory encryption using Intel Total Memory Encryption (TME).\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-database-migration-service","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-database-migration-service","description":"general:products/aws-database-migration-service","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:54:46+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:54:46+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#developer-tools","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"developer-tools","description":"marketing:marchitecture/developer-tools","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:40:35+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#databases","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"databases","description":"marketing:marchitecture/databases","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-08-amazon-aurora-supports-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/08/amazon-aurora-supports-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:54:24+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:22:46+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Aurora\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;now offers customers the option to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses in their Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) on new and existing Amazon Aurora instances. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their databases on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThe continued growth of the internet is exhausting available Internet Protocol version 4 (IPv4) addresses. IPv6 increases the number of available addresses by several orders of magnitude and customers no longer need to manage overlapping address spaces in their VPCs. Customers can standardize their applications on the new version of Internet Protocol by moving to IPv6 with a few clicks in the AWS Management Console.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eSupport for IPv6 on Amazon Aurora is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) regions where Amazon Aurora is available. See \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;for a full listing of our Regions. Get started with the AWS CLI or \u003ca href\u003d\"https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/home\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Management Console\u003c/a\u003e. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eTo learn more about configuring your environment for IPv6, please refer to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/USER_VPC.WorkingWithRDSInstanceinaVPC.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eIPv6 User Guide\u003c/a\u003e. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-09-15T17:19:44Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/08/amazon-aurora-supports-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2022-08-17T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon Aurora now supports Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"/content/aws.amazon.com/live/en_US/rds/aurora.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAmazon Aurora\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;now offers customers the option to use Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) addresses in their Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) on new and existing Amazon Aurora instances. Customers moving to IPv6 can simplify their network stack by running their databases on a network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-aurora","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-aurora","description":"general:products/amazon-aurora","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T22:36:01+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T22:36:01+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#databases","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"databases","description":"marketing:marchitecture/databases","dateCreated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-07T18:00:12+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-07-aws-global-accelerator-announces-ipv6-support","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/07/aws-global-accelerator-announces-ipv6-support/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:51:03+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:23:41+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Global Accelerator now offers dual-stack accelerators that enable you to route IPv6 traffic to Regional Application Load Balancer endpoints. Starting today, you can get the availability, security and performance benefits of AWS Global Accelerator for both IPv4 and IPv6 traffic while routing traffic towards Application Load Balancer endpoints.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith dual-stack accelerators, you can route IPv6 traffic, together with IPv4 traffic, towards dual-stack Application Load Balancer endpoints.  With dual-stack accelerators, you will get two static IPv6 addresses, in addition to the two static IPv4 addresses. You can choose a dual-stack accelerator when you set up a new accelerator, or you can update an existing IPv4-only accelerator to dual-stack, using standard AWS tools, including the AWS Management Console, AWS SDK, or the AWS CLI. When you update an existing accelerator, there\u0027s no change to the IPv4 specifications of the accelerator. You can monitor your IPv6 traffic in addition to the IPv4 traffic with existing Global Accelerator CloudWatch metrics.\u0026nbsp;\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThere is no additional charge when you use dual-stack accelerators in Global Accelerator. To learn more, see the\u0026nbsp;\u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/latest/dg/what-is-global-accelerator.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edeveloper documentation\u003c/a\u003e in the AWS Global Accelerator Developer Guide. To get started with AWS Global Accelerator, visit the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/global-accelerator/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eGlobal Accelerator product page\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-09-15T17:21:15Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/07/aws-global-accelerator-announces-ipv6-support/","postDateTime":"2022-07-28T04:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS Global Accelerator announces IPv6 support","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Global Accelerator now offers dual-stack accelerators that enable you to route IPv6 traffic to Regional Application Load Balancer endpoints. Starting today, you can get the availability, security and performance benefits of AWS Global Accelerator for both IPv4 and IPv6 traffic while routing traffic towards Application Load Balancer endpoints.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-global-accelerator","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-global-accelerator","description":"general:products/aws-global-accelerator","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:54:44+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:54:44+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-05-aws-app-mesh-supports-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/aws-app-mesh-supports-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:52:26+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:24:27+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAWS App Mesh now supports IPv6 allowing customers to support workloads running in IPv6 networks and to invoke App Mesh APIs over IPv6. This helps customers to meet IPv6 compliance requirements, and removes the need for expensive networking equipment to handle address translation between IPv4 and IPv6. AWS App Mesh is a service mesh that provides application-level networking to make it easier for your services to communicate with each other across multiple types of compute infrastructure. AWS App Mesh standardizes how your services communicate, giving you end-to-end visibility and options to tune for high-availability of your applications.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith this new capability, you can configure your mesh to support workloads in IPv6 networks and use AWS App Mesh’s new dual-stack endpoints which support both IPv4 and IPv6. The new dual stack endpoints in AWS App Mesh have the format appmesh.region.api.aws. For example, the dual-stack endpoint in US East (N. Virginia) is appmesh.us-east-1.api.aws. When you make a request to a dual-stack App Mesh endpoint, the endpoint resolves to an IPv6 or an IPv4 address, depending on the protocol used by your network and client.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the new IPv6 network support with AWS App Mesh at no additional cost. For more information on AWS App Mesh region availability, please see the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS Region table\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eFor more information, please visit the AWS App Mesh \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/app-mesh/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eproduct page\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/app-mesh/index.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003edocumentation\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-09-15T17:23:25Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/aws-app-mesh-supports-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2022-05-18T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS App Mesh now supports Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAWS App Mesh now supports IPv6 allowing customers to support workloads running in IPv6 networks and to invoke App Mesh APIs over IPv6. This helps customers to meet IPv6 compliance requirements, and removes the need for expensive networking equipment to handle address translation between IPv4 and IPv6. AWS App Mesh is a service mesh that provides application-level networking to make it easier for your services to communicate with each other across multiple types of compute infrastructure. AWS App Mesh standardizes how your services communicate, giving you end-to-end visibility and options to tune for high-availability of your applications.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#containers","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"containers","description":"marketing:marchitecture/containers","dateCreated":"2023-11-08T18:48:39+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-08T18:48:39+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-app-mesh","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-app-mesh","description":"general:products/aws-app-mesh","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:53:41+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-05-amazon-vpc-multiple-ipv6-cidr-blocks","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/amazon-vpc-multiple-ipv6-cidr-blocks/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:49:26+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:21:07+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Web Services (AWS) announces the launch of multiple IPv6 classless inter-domain routing (CIDR) blocks in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), enabling customer to attach up to 5 prefixes to their VPCs. Before today, customers could add up to 5 IPv4 CIDR blocks and 1 IPv6 block. With this new feature, customers can now use multiple blocks to build logical separation within their VPCs with independent CIDR blocks. CIDR blocks can be associated from the Amazon provided pool and/or a pool of bring-your-own IPv6 addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith IPv4 customers needed additional CIDR blocks to increase their address space within a VPC.  With IPv6, address space should no longer be a concern.  Customers can use multiple CIDR blocks to better segment their network infrastructure such as subnets, route tables, and security groups with different prefix ranges for different applications.  With the ability to BYOIP, customers can launch one subnet from their own BYOIPv6 pool and another subnet with Amazon provided IP addresses. With BYOIP pools having the option to not advertise to the internet, customers can use these separate CIDRs to achieve clear separation of internal and external facing applications in their VPCs. \u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eThis feature is available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no additional cost. For more information, please visit the VPC \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003euser guide\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-09-15T17:33:34Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/amazon-vpc-multiple-ipv6-cidr-blocks/","postDateTime":"2022-05-13T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"Amazon VPC now supports multiple IPv6 CIDR blocks","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Web Services (AWS) announces the launch of multiple IPv6 classless inter-domain routing (CIDR) blocks in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), enabling customer to attach up to 5 prefixes to their VPCs. Before today, customers could add up to 5 IPv4 CIDR blocks and 1 IPv6 block. With this new feature, customers can now use multiple blocks to build logical separation within their VPCs with independent CIDR blocks. CIDR blocks can be associated from the Amazon provided pool and/or a pool of bring-your-own IPv6 addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#amazon-vpc","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"amazon-vpc","description":"general:products/amazon-vpc","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:18+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]},{"item":{"id":"whats-new-v2#about-aws-whats-new-2022-05-aws-privatelink-ipv6","locale":"en_US","directoryId":"whats-new-v2","name":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/aws-privatelink-ipv6/","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:48:01+0000","dateUpdated":"2024-07-25T14:25:22+0000","additionalFields":{"postBody":"\u003cp\u003eYou can now connect over IPv6 to your services hosted in AWS using AWS PrivateLink. AWS PrivateLink is a highly available, scalable technology that enables you to privately connect your VPC to supported AWS services, services hosted by other AWS accounts (VPC Endpoint Services), third-party SaaS services and supported AWS Marketplace partner services. \u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eWith this launch, you can now easily enable IPv6 access to your existing IPv4-based or IPv6-based services via AWS PrivateLink by updating flags in your VPC Endpoint Service and Network Load Balancer (NLB) configurations. If you are a PrivateLink Endpoint owner, you can now access IPv6-enabled PrivateLink services by upgrading your existing IPv4-based endpoints to dual-stack or creating new PrivateLink endpoints in IPv6-only subnets. \u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\u003cp\u003eIPv6 is supported only for PrivateLink Services and Endpoints of type “Interface” and not for type “Gateway Load Balancer”. It is available in all public AWS \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eRegions\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;and GovCloud Regions. To learn more, read our \u003ca href\u003d\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/expedite-your-ipv6-adoption-with-privatelink-services-and-endpoints/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003elaunch blog\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;or visit \u003ca href\u003d\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/what-is-privatelink.html\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noopener\"\u003eAWS PrivateLink\u003c/a\u003e\u0026nbsp;in the Amazon VPC Developer Guide.\u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","modifiedDate":"2022-09-15T18:00:43Z","headlineUrl":"/about-aws/whats-new/2022/05/aws-privatelink-ipv6/","postDateTime":"2022-05-11T07:00:00Z","contentType":"Launch Announcement","headline":"AWS PrivateLink announces support for IPv6","postSummary":"\u003cp\u003eYou can now connect over IPv6 to your services hosted in AWS using AWS PrivateLink. AWS PrivateLink is a highly available, scalable technology that enables you to privately connect your VPC to supported AWS services, services hosted by other AWS accounts (VPC Endpoint Services), third-party SaaS services and supported AWS Marketplace partner services. \u003cbr\u003e\r\n\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"}},"tags":[{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#aws-privatelink","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"aws-privatelink","description":"general:products/aws-privatelink","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:51:16+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture#networking-and-content-delivery","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#marketing-marchitecture","name":"networking-and-content-delivery","description":"marketing:marchitecture/networking-and-content-delivery","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T13:50:17+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#year#2022","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#year","name":"2022","description":"year#2022","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:33:49+0000"},{"id":"GLOBAL#content-type#launch-announcement","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"GLOBAL#content-type","name":"Launch Announcement","description":"Launch Announcement","dateCreated":"2020-04-29T05:19:27+0000","dateUpdated":"2022-02-03T03:31:08+0000"},{"id":"whats-new-v2#general-products#ipv6-at-aws","locale":"en_US","tagNamespaceId":"whats-new-v2#general-products","name":"ipv6-at-aws","description":"general:products/ipv6-at-aws","dateCreated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000","dateUpdated":"2023-11-09T14:44:04+0000"}]}],"metadata":{"count":28,"totalHits":28},"fieldTypes":{"regionalAvailability":"LongText","nofollow":"Boolean","date":"Date","language":"Text","title":"Text","postDateTime":"DateTime","body":"Text","postSummary":"LongText","ctaLabel":"Text","mediaThumbnail":"Text","nosnippet":"Boolean","price":"Text","ctaLink":"URL","headline":"Text","contentType":"Text","bodyBack":"LongText","secondaryCTALabel":"Text","primaryCTALink":"URL","noindex":"Boolean","level":"Text","heading":"Text","mediaType":"Text","mediaAlt":"Text","headlineUrl":"Text","mediaSrc":"URL","durationMinutes":"Number","badge":"JSON","videoImageUrl":"URL","postBody":"LongText","secondaryCTALink":"URL","Video":"URL","modifiedDate":"DateTime","namePerson":"Text","location":"Text","time":"Text","publishedDate":"DateTime","durationReadtime":"Number","primaryCTALabel":"Text"}}